1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Section 3

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,466
KATIE:

3
00:00:02,466 --> 00:00:07,980
Joe, you know I'm giving a presentation in our film studies class next week?

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:08,724
JOE:

5
00:00:08,724 --> 00:00:08,910
Yes.

6
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:09,533
KATIE:

7
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,201
Well, could we discuss it?

8
00:00:11,201 --> 00:00:14,900
I could do with getting someone else's opinion.

9
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:14,950
JOE:

10
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:15,961
Of course.

11
00:00:15,961 --> 00:00:16,315
Katie.

12
00:00:16,315 --> 00:00:18,790
What are you going to talk about ?

13
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:19,317
KATIE:

14
00:00:19,317 --> 00:00:23,007
It's about film adaptations of Shakespeare's plays.

15
00:00:23,007 --> 00:00:29,860
I've got very interested in all the different approaches that film directors take .

16
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:30,535
JOE:

17
00:00:30,535 --> 00:00:31,210
Uhuh .

18
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:31,676
KATIE:

19
00:00:31,676 --> 00:00:34,935
So I thought I'd start with Giannetti,

20
00:00:34,935 --> 00:00:38,194
who's a professor of film and literature,

21
00:00:38,194 --> 00:00:44,712
and in one of his books he came up with a straightforward classification of

22
00:00:44,712 --> 00:00:51,230
film adaptations based on how faithful they are to the original plays and novels.

23
00:00:51,230 --> 00:00:52,020
JOE:

24
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:52,810
Right.

25
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:53,236
KATIE:

26
00:00:53,236 --> 00:00:56,221
I've already made some notes on that,

27
00:00:56,221 --> 00:01:00,910
so I just need to sort those out before the presentation.

28
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:07,466
I thought that next I'd ask the class to come up with the worst

29
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:10,744
examples of Shakespeare adaptations that they've seen,

30
00:01:10,744 --> 00:01:12,617
and to say why.

31
00:01:12,617 --> 00:01:17,300
That should be more fun than having their favourite versions.

32
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:17,767
JOE:

33
00:01:17,767 --> 00:01:21,500
Yes, I can certainly think of a couple!

34
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:21,995
KATIE:

35
00:01:21,995 --> 00:01:22,489
Right.

36
00:01:22,489 --> 00:01:26,447
Next I want to talk about Rachel Malchow.

37
00:01:26,447 --> 00:01:32,879
I came across something on the internet about her work on film adaptations,

38
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:39,310
and I was thinking of showing some film clips to illustrate her ideas.

39
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:39,753
JOE:

40
00:01:39,753 --> 00:01:42,410
Will you have enough time, though?

41
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:45,510
Both to prepare and during the presentation?

42
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:50,195
After all, I doubt if you'll be able to find all the clips you

43
00:01:50,195 --> 00:01:50,530
want.

44
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:51,123
KATIE:

45
00:01:51,123 --> 00:01:51,717
Hmm.

46
00:01:51,717 --> 00:01:53,497
Perhaps you're right.

47
00:01:53,497 --> 00:02:01,806
OK, well, I'd better do some slides instead, saying how various films relate to

48
00:02:01,806 --> 00:02:03,586
what she says.

49
00:02:03,586 --> 00:02:05,960
That should encourage discussion.

50
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:06,820
JOE:

51
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:07,680
Mmm.

52
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:08,146
KATIE:

53
00:02:08,146 --> 00:02:14,676
Next I want to say something about how plays may be chosen for adaptation

54
00:02:14,676 --> 00:02:20,740
because they're concerned with issues of the time when the film is made.

55
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:21,171
JOE:

56
00:02:21,171 --> 00:02:23,325
You mean things like patriotism,

57
00:02:23,325 --> 00:02:25,480
or the role of governments?

58
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:25,995
KATIE:

59
00:02:25,995 --> 00:02:26,511
Exactly.

60
00:02:26,511 --> 00:02:28,057
It's quite tricky,

61
00:02:28,057 --> 00:02:33,210
but I've got a few ideas I'd like to discuss.

62
00:02:33,210 --> 00:02:33,680
KATIE:

63
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:40,255
And finally I want to talk about a few adaptations that I think illustrate

64
00:02:40,255 --> 00:02:42,134
a range of approaches,

65
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:44,952
and make some comments on them.

66
00:02:44,952 --> 00:02:48,240
Do you know the Japanese film Ran?

67
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:48,679
JOE:

68
00:02:48,679 --> 00:02:50,436
I haven't seen it.

69
00:02:50,436 --> 00:02:54,390
It was based on Shakespeare's King Lear, wasn't it?

70
00:02:54,390 --> 00:02:54,937
KATIE:

71
00:02:54,937 --> 00:02:56,030
That's right.

72
00:02:56,030 --> 00:03:02,590
It was a very loose adaptation using the same situation and story,

73
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:09,150
but moving it to 16th century Japan instead of 16th century Britain.

74
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:13,523
So for example the king's daughters become sons,

75
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:20,630
because in Japanese culture at that time, women couldn't succeed to the throne.

76
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:21,030
JOE:

77
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:21,430
OK.

78
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:27,030
I hope you're going to talk about the 1993 film of Much Ado About

79
00:03:27,030 --> 00:03:27,430
Nothing.

80
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:31,030
I think that's one of the best Shakespeare films.

81
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:34,630
It really brings the play to life, doesn't it?

82
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:35,005
KATIE:

83
00:03:35,005 --> 00:03:36,131
Yes, I agree.

84
00:03:36,131 --> 00:03:41,383
And I think filming it in Italy, where the play is set, makes you

85
00:03:41,383 --> 00:03:45,510
see what life was like at the time of the play.

86
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:46,310
JOE:

87
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:47,110
Absolutely.

88
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:49,510
Right, what's next?

89
00:03:49,510 --> 00:03:50,041
KATIE:

90
00:03:50,041 --> 00:03:54,816
Er, next, I thought Romeo & Juliet , the 1996 film,

91
00:03:54,816 --> 00:03:59,060
which moves the action into the present day.

92
00:03:59,060 --> 00:03:59,525
JOE:

93
00:03:59,525 --> 00:04:05,574
Yes, it worked really well, I thought - changing the two feuding families in

94
00:04:05,574 --> 00:04:12,089
the original to two competing business empires, even though they're speaking in the English

95
00:04:12,089 --> 00:04:13,950
of the original play.

96
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:14,375
KATIE:

97
00:04:14,375 --> 00:04:17,348
You'd expect it would sound really bizarre,

98
00:04:17,348 --> 00:04:21,170
but I found I soon got used to it.

99
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:21,790
JOE:

100
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:23,030
Me too.

101
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:23,534
KATIE:

102
00:04:23,534 --> 00:04:30,597
Then I thought I'd include a real Hollywood film, one that's intended to appeal

103
00:04:30,597 --> 00:04:33,120
to a mass commercial audience.

104
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:33,459
JOE:

105
00:04:33,459 --> 00:04:36,170
There must be quite a number of those.

106
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:36,698
KATIE:

107
00:04:36,698 --> 00:04:37,227
Yes,

108
00:04:37,227 --> 00:04:41,455
but I've picked the 1996 film of Hamlet.

109
00:04:41,455 --> 00:04:45,154
It included every line of the text,

110
00:04:45,154 --> 00:04:52,024
but it's more like a typical action hero movie - there are loads of

111
00:04:52,024 --> 00:04:53,081
special effects,

112
00:04:53,081 --> 00:04:56,780
but no unifying interpretation of the play.

113
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:57,295
JOE:

114
00:04:57,295 --> 00:04:59,870
All show and no substance.

115
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:00,438
KATIE:

116
00:05:00,438 --> 00:05:01,006
Exactly.

117
00:05:01,006 --> 00:05:05,550
Then there's Prospero's Books, based on The Tempest.

118
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:11,230
That was really innovative, from a stylistic point of view.

119
00:05:11,230 --> 00:05:11,675
JOE:

120
00:05:11,675 --> 00:05:17,460
Didn't it include dance and singing and animation, as well as live actors?

121
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:17,907
KATIE:

122
00:05:17,907 --> 00:05:19,248
Yes, it did.

123
00:05:19,248 --> 00:05:22,825
I also want to mention Looking for Richard.

124
00:05:22,825 --> 00:05:25,060
Did you ever see it?

125
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:25,446
JOE:

126
00:05:25,446 --> 00:05:25,832
No.

127
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:27,763
but I've read about it.

128
00:05:27,763 --> 00:05:33,168
It was a blend of a documentary with a few scenes from Richard lll,

129
00:05:33,168 --> 00:05:33,940
wasn't it?

130
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:34,365
KATIE:

131
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:35,216
That's right.

132
00:05:35,216 --> 00:05:41,170
It's more a way of looking into how people nowadays connect with the playwright

133
00:05:41,170 --> 00:05:44,573
- the play is really just the starting point.

134
00:05:44,573 --> 00:05:47,550
And that' ll be where I finish.

135
00:05:47,550 --> 00:05:47,996
JOE:

136
00:05:47,996 --> 00:05:52,010
Well, it sounds as though it'll be very interesting.
